{
 "lead": "A light wave in a fiber has two speeds. The crests of the carrier move at the phase velocity; the envelope of a pulse, which carries the bits, moves at the group velocity. Link timing, the delay of a bit from end to end and the spreading of pulses all follow the group velocity. The two speeds differ because the effective index changes with wavelength, and the group index N<sub>g</sub> captures that difference in one number. Only a few universities teach this topic in detail, but delay calculations from it are common short questions.",
 "prereq": "You need: the propagation constant β and effective index from <a href=\"#electromagnetic-mode-theory-modes-in-planar-guides\">Electromagnetic mode theory; modes in planar guides</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Phase velocity: how fast the crests move",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A guided mode at one frequency varies as e<sup>j(ωt − βz)</sup>. A point of constant phase, a crest, satisfies ωt − βz = constant, so it moves at"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "v<sub>p</sub> = ω/β = c/n<sub>eff</sub>",
     "where": "v<sub>p</sub> is the phase velocity in m/s; ω the angular frequency in rad/s; β the propagation constant in rad/m; c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s; n<sub>eff</sub> = β/k₀ the mode's effective index. For a plane wave in bulk glass n<sub>eff</sub> is just n."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A single unchanging frequency carries no information: it has no start, no end and no bits. Information needs modulation, and a modulated signal contains a band of frequencies."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Group velocity and the group index",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Two waves at nearby frequencies ω ± Δω add to a carrier inside a slowly varying envelope. The crests travel at ω/β, but the envelope peak, where the two waves stay in step, travels at Δω/Δβ. In the limit this is the <b>group velocity</b>:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "v<sub>g</sub> = dω/dβ",
     "where": "the slope of the ω–β curve at the carrier frequency."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Write β = 2πn<sub>eff</sub>/λ and differentiate with respect to ω (using dλ/dω = −λ²/2πc). The result is 1/v<sub>g</sub> = dβ/dω = (1/c)(n<sub>eff</sub> − λ dn<sub>eff</sub>/dλ), so"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>v<sub>g</sub> = c/N<sub>g</sub>,   N<sub>g</sub> = n<sub>eff</sub> − λ dn<sub>eff</sub>/dλ</b><br>N<sub>g</sub> is the group index (dimensionless); dn<sub>eff</sub>/dλ is the slope of the effective index with wavelength. In silica near 1550 nm the slope is negative, so N<sub>g</sub> is larger than n<sub>eff</sub> and pulses travel slower than crests."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A pulse launched into a fiber of length L arrives after the <b>group delay</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "τ = L/v<sub>g</sub> = L · N<sub>g</sub> / c",
     "where": "τ in seconds, L in metres. This delay, not L/v<sub>p</sub>, is what a timing or rise-time budget uses."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "If N<sub>g</sub> itself changes with wavelength, the different wavelengths in a pulse arrive at different times and the pulse spreads. That is chromatic dispersion, which comes with signal degradation in fibers (<i>Chromatic dispersion: material and waveguide</i>)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Reading the numbers",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In silica fibers N<sub>g</sub> and n<sub>eff</sub> differ by only 1 to 2 %, so v<sub>p</sub> and v<sub>g</sub> are close, but over long spans the difference adds up; the worked example finds it for 10 km. With N<sub>g</sub> near 1.48, each kilometre of fiber delays a pulse by about 4.9 µs, a number used when timing distant sites."
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "Use v<sub>g</sub> and N<sub>g</sub> for anything that carries bits: delay, bit slots, synchronisation, dispersion.",
      "Use v<sub>p</sub> and n<sub>eff</sub> for phase: interference, Bragg gratings, the phase shift in a modulator arm.",
      "Both change with waveguide geometry as well as with the glass, because n<sub>eff</sub> depends on how much of the mode runs in the cladding."
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "At λ = 1550 nm a fiber mode has n<sub>eff</sub> = 1.46 and N<sub>g</sub> = 1.48. With c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s, find (a) the phase velocity, (b) the group velocity, (c) the pulse delay over 10 km and how far the envelope lags the crests.",
   "steps": [
    "(a) v<sub>p</sub> = c/n<sub>eff</sub> = 3.00 × 10⁸/1.46 = 2.055 × 10⁸ m/s.",
    "(b) v<sub>g</sub> = c/N<sub>g</sub> = 3.00 × 10⁸/1.48 = 2.027 × 10⁸ m/s.",
    "(c) L = 10 km = 1.0 × 10⁴ m. τ = L/v<sub>g</sub> = 10⁴/(2.027 × 10⁸) = 4.933 × 10⁻⁵ s = 49.33 µs.",
    "Crest time L/v<sub>p</sub> = 10⁴ × 1.46/(3.00 × 10⁸) = 48.67 µs, so the envelope lags by 49.33 − 48.67 = 0.67 µs.",
    "Check: the lag equals L(N<sub>g</sub> − n<sub>eff</sub>)/c = 10⁴ × 0.02/(3.00 × 10⁸) = 0.67 µs."
   ],
   "answer": "v<sub>p</sub> = 2.05 × 10⁸ m/s, v<sub>g</sub> = 2.03 × 10⁸ m/s; the pulse takes 49.3 µs over 10 km, 0.67 µs behind the crests."
  },
  {
   "question": "Near 1550 nm a fiber's effective index is 1.444 and its slope is dn<sub>eff</sub>/dλ = −0.012 per µm. Find N<sub>g</sub>, v<sub>g</sub>, and the group delay over 50 km.",
   "steps": [
    "λ dn<sub>eff</sub>/dλ = 1.55 µm × (−0.012 µm⁻¹) = −0.0186 (the µm cancels).",
    "N<sub>g</sub> = 1.444 − (−0.0186) = 1.4626.",
    "v<sub>g</sub> = 3.00 × 10⁸/1.4626 = 2.051 × 10⁸ m/s.",
    "τ = L N<sub>g</sub>/c = 5.0 × 10⁴ × 1.4626/(3.00 × 10⁸) = 2.438 × 10⁻⁴ s = 243.8 µs.",
    "Check: N<sub>g</sub> &gt; n<sub>eff</sub>, as expected for a negative slope."
   ],
   "answer": "N<sub>g</sub> = 1.463, v<sub>g</sub> = 2.05 × 10⁸ m/s and the 50 km delay is 244 µs."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Using n<sub>eff</sub> instead of N<sub>g</sub> in τ = LN<sub>g</sub>/c understates the delay; pulses follow the group index.",
  "Phase velocity is not the speed of information; a single unmodulated frequency carries no bits.",
  "N<sub>g</sub> = n<sub>eff</sub> − λ dn<sub>eff</sub>/dλ: with a negative slope the two minus signs make N<sub>g</sub> larger. Getting N<sub>g</sub> below n<sub>eff</sub> in silica points to a sign slip.",
  "Put L in metres: 10 km is 1.0 × 10⁴ m. Keep λ and the slope in matching units (µm and per µm) so the product is a pure number."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Define phase velocity and group velocity in terms of ω and β.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "v<sub>p</sub> = ω/β, the speed of a point of constant phase; v<sub>g</sub> = dω/dβ, the speed of the envelope."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Write the group index in terms of n<sub>eff</sub> and λ.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "N<sub>g</sub> = n<sub>eff</sub> − λ dn<sub>eff</sub>/dλ, dimensionless; v<sub>g</sub> = c/N<sub>g</sub>."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does a pulse travel at the group velocity rather than the phase velocity?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "A pulse is a band of frequencies. Its envelope sits where these components stay in step, and that point moves at dω/dβ; the crests inside the envelope move at ω/β and slide through it."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A fiber has N<sub>g</sub> = 1.47. Find the group delay over 2 km.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "v<sub>g</sub> = 3.00 × 10⁸/1.47 = 2.041 × 10⁸ m/s; τ = 2000/(2.041 × 10⁸) = 9.80 × 10⁻⁶ s = 9.80 µs."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "At 1310 nm, n<sub>eff</sub> = 1.447 and dn<sub>eff</sub>/dλ = −0.015 per µm. Find N<sub>g</sub>.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "λ dn<sub>eff</sub>/dλ = 1.31 × (−0.015) = −0.01965; N<sub>g</sub> = 1.447 + 0.01965 = 1.4667."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Two fibers share n<sub>eff</sub> = 1.45 but have N<sub>g</sub> = 1.46 and 1.49. Which delays a pulse more over 1 km, and by how much?",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "τ = LN<sub>g</sub>/c, so the N<sub>g</sub> = 1.49 fiber: 4.967 µs against 4.867 µs, 0.10 µs more per km. Their equal n<sub>eff</sub> does not matter for pulse timing."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. Group delay is what dispersion test sets measure: the phase-shift method reads the delay at several wavelengths and differentiates it. Slow-light structures such as photonic-crystal waveguides raise N<sub>g</sub> to tens or more to strengthen light–matter interaction, and solitons balance dispersion against the fiber's nonlinearity (see <i>Nonlinear effects: self-phase modulation and solitons</i>). Search next for the Sellmeier equation, group delay measurement and slow light.",
